WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.084
♪♪

00:00:01.084 --> 00:00:02.085
Ambition.

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:03.128
♪♪

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:04.129
Greed.

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:05.088
♪♪

00:00:05.088 --> 00:00:06.840
And a guy named Willy.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.133
Willy Shakespeare.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:13.889
♪♪

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:20.437
Witch:
By the pricking of my thumbs,
something wicked this way comes.

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:22.231
♪♪

00:00:22.231 --> 00:00:23.607
Our version of MacBeth

00:00:23.607 --> 00:00:26.026
Mary Zophres:
made Shakespeare accessible.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:29.613
The way I've always looked at
the film, is that it doesn't
take place in any physical place.

00:00:29.613 --> 00:00:32.032
Carter Burwell:
It takes place in the minds of
the characters

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:33.742
Carter Burwell:
and the psychological reality.

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:37.496
Macbeth has been done in many
forms and many time periods.

00:00:37.496 --> 00:00:40.249
Mary Zophres:
But we're inspired by Joel's
vision.

00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:43.585
Mary Zophres:
Which was this highly defined
black and white

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:46.880
Mary Zophres:
and very geometric and very
architectural.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.551
And that's what helped us come
up with a set of rules on
MacBeth.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:54.429
What's fascinating is, I don't
know, I think it's the first
time I've worked with

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:55.847
a husband and wife team.

00:00:56.306 --> 00:00:58.308
So, that was, that was
interesting.

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:00.727
Just watching the two of them,
how they operate.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:03.897
Denzel Washington:
They're two halves of the same
person, I mean they rely on each

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.149
Denzel Washington:
other and, uh, trust each
other.

00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:09.987
They're both brilliant,
brilliant artists and care about
the work.

00:01:10.362 --> 00:01:14.199
Ellen Chenoweth:
Joel really wanted the language
to be very specific and very clear.

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:18.704
Ellen Chenoweth:
And he really wanted the actors
and their ability to pull these scenes off.

00:01:18.704 --> 00:01:23.333
And make the language sing and
make it real at the same time.

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:27.838
If chance will have me king,
why, chance may crown me without
my stir.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:28.797
(Sword chopping)

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:30.549
Come what come may.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.678
It's important for Joel, in
films, to usually establish
visual themes,

00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:37.973
Carter Burwell:
as well as verbal
themes and auditory themes.

00:01:37.973 --> 00:01:40.851
Denzel Washington:
He's just brilliant. He's one of
the most, if not the most, 

00:01:40.851 --> 00:01:43.729
brilliant filmmaker I've worked
with, period.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:48.900
There are a lot of things about
working with Joel that are
unique,

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.069
compared to working with other
filmmakers.

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:56.074
Joel is known for really
preparing well for pretty much
the whole process.

00:01:56.074 --> 00:02:00.454
He had the story and he had the
visuals already in his head.

00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:04.916
Seeing how Joel saw it was a
masterclass in, in filmmaking.

00:02:05.292 --> 00:02:06.752
(Thunderous knocking)

00:02:06.752 --> 00:02:10.881
♪♪

00:02:10.881 --> 00:02:14.134
I had done a number of movies
with Joel and Ethan.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.888
Ellen Chenoweth:
So, I had a pretty good sense of
what kinds of actors he liked 

00:02:17.888 --> 00:02:21.141
Ellen Chenoweth:
and at this point, we had
Frances McDormand.

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:24.519
Ellen Chenoweth:
And once you start thinking
about who can play opposite her,

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:30.817
and hold their own and has had
classical training, it was a
very, very short list.

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:34.154
Ellen Chenoweth:
And then, when Denzel said he
would do it, it was kind of a miracle

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:38.241
because he's classically trained
and he's a huge movie star.

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:40.160
My dearest love...

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.455
I mean, he's a giant so that
was, kind of, a dream come true.

00:02:43.455 --> 00:02:45.290
...Duncan comes here tonight.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.752
Lady Macbeth:
Look like the innocent flower,
but be the serpent under it.

00:02:48.752 --> 00:02:49.961
Leave all the rest to me.

00:02:50.921 --> 00:02:53.131
She was amazing.

00:02:53.131 --> 00:02:54.174
Amazing.

00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:56.051
Denzel Washington:
She's brilliant, really,

00:02:56.051 --> 00:02:59.096
Denzel Washington:
and a lovely, lovely woman.
Lovely woman.

00:02:59.096 --> 00:03:00.597
(Thunderous knocking)

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:04.893
♪♪

00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:06.645
 There was no color on set. 

00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:12.692
There was only a dress or two
that Frances McDormand was
wearing, which were in color.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.778
But otherwise, everything
was black and white.

00:03:14.778 --> 00:03:20.450
Bruno Delbonnel:
So, when the actors came on set,
they were really aware of what it looks like.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.079
All was in black and white, all
on sound stages.

00:03:25.664 --> 00:03:27.541
Carter Burwell:
We just got these very
exaggerated 

00:03:27.541 --> 00:03:30.836
Carter Burwell:
abstractions sometimes that come
from the sets.

00:03:30.836 --> 00:03:33.338
It was, kind of, obvious it has
to be black and white.

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:37.884
Bruno Delbonnel:
Because black and white already
introduced some, kind of, abstraction, you see.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:42.806
It's a... you're not
connected to reality, in some
ways, with black and white.

00:03:42.806 --> 00:03:44.349
You have already a distance.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:48.895
Witch:
Double, double, toil and
trouble,

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:53.066
fire burn and cauldron bubble.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:54.067
(Splash)

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.278
First Apparition:
Macbeth, Macbeth...

00:03:56.278 --> 00:03:57.362
Macbeth.

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:00.782
With the designs of the sets
being so abstract,

00:04:00.782 --> 00:04:05.495
Stefan Dechant:
I never felt the need that
I needed to have any muted color, at all.

00:04:05.495 --> 00:04:10.041
And just to move quickly, it was
just easier to paint in grays.

00:04:10.041 --> 00:04:14.087
I've done another black and
white movie, and for hair, what
you wanna do, is you're

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.464
Cydney Cornell:
not dealing with color.

00:04:15.464 --> 00:04:18.717
Cydney Cornell:
So, you wanna have shadows and
reflection.

00:04:18.717 --> 00:04:21.386
Cydney Cornell:
And then those are your things 
you're working with.

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:24.306
Cydney Cornell:
Light or dark, shiny or not
shiny.

00:04:24.306 --> 00:04:26.224
Designing a film that's in
black and white

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:28.435
was hugely important.

00:04:28.435 --> 00:04:32.314
Mary Zophres:
Texture and textiles and
embroidery and

00:04:32.314 --> 00:04:34.816
Mary Zophres:
layering and aging.

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:38.820
All these different tools worked
really well in this form of
black and white.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.074
Will all great Neptune's ocean
wash this blood clean from my
hand?

00:04:43.074 --> 00:04:44.951
♪♪

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:47.746
Bruno Delbonnel:
It always came to the way I
would light it.

00:04:48.538 --> 00:04:51.249
Bruno Delbonnel:
We never really know if it's day
or night.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:53.293
Bruno Delbonnel:
and it's really in the play.

00:04:54.336 --> 00:04:59.216
Bruno Delbonnel:
So, what if we play with this
idea of not knowing,

00:04:59.216 --> 00:05:03.637
Bruno Delbonnel:
or is there a transition that
you can see going from day to night?

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:08.058
It was a fair thing just to be,
kind of, ambiguous with the
light.

00:05:08.058 --> 00:05:10.143
Bruno Delbonnel:
The shadows are very sharp.

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:14.606
Bruno Delbonnel:
So, when I use theater light, in
fact, I didn't use any movie light,

00:05:14.606 --> 00:05:18.068
it was the light you use for
a rock and roll concert.

00:05:18.068 --> 00:05:22.489
Things without all remedy should
be without regard, what's done
is done.

00:05:22.906 --> 00:05:25.951
We have scorched the snake, not
killed it.

00:05:25.951 --> 00:05:27.577
(Thunderous knocking)

00:05:27.577 --> 00:05:32.207
♪♪

00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:35.126
Denzel Washington:
The way the film, that Joel
brilliantly made,

00:05:35.126 --> 00:05:37.337
Denzel Washington:
that Frances brilliantly played,

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:39.673
it moves.

00:05:39.673 --> 00:05:41.091
(Wind blows)

00:05:41.091 --> 00:05:45.637
It really moves. You understand
what it is and he took his time
and got it right.

00:05:46.137 --> 00:05:49.891
Alex Lemke:
Joel talked to us about how he
wanted to make Macbeth into a film,

00:05:49.891 --> 00:05:52.352
Alex Lemke:
while preserving the theatrical
experience of it.

00:05:52.352 --> 00:06:00.694
So, he was going to use film
influences, mainly from
German Expressionism, to turn this into a, uh, film.

00:06:00.694 --> 00:06:01.444
(Horse neighs)

00:06:01.444 --> 00:06:04.114
The format choice that the
movie was shot in,

00:06:04.114 --> 00:06:08.076
Michael Huber:
2D backdrops that kind of looked
like backdrops in the real theaters,

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:13.832
and all the artistic choices
that were made, would lead to a
little bit of a, um, abstraction.

00:06:13.832 --> 00:06:19.254
Especially in the sequences
where we're not entirely sure
what is reality and what is not,

00:06:19.254 --> 00:06:21.965
adds to that level of uh, stage
performance.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:24.718
Ahh!

00:06:26.011 --> 00:06:31.891
So, it's a combination of
backdrops, painted backings,
nets, screens and stuff like that.

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:33.727
Are you a man?

00:06:33.727 --> 00:06:38.607
Ay, and a bold one, that dare
look upon that which might
appall the devil.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.694
Bruno Delbonnel:
The academy frame was about the
power of the close-up.

00:06:43.278 --> 00:06:45.238
It's a fabulous format.

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:50.327
And, you know, seriously, when
you have Denzel's face, you know
academy fills the frame and,

00:06:50.327 --> 00:06:52.537
Bruno Delbonnel:
ah, it's, it's already very
strong

00:06:53.455 --> 00:06:57.584
Bruno Delbonnel:
and Frances is the same and has
such a presence.

00:06:57.584 --> 00:07:03.423
Being in the academy framing and
being able to be in dialogue and
to draw with Joel.

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:06.760
Stefan Decant:
You could design the sets for
those parameters.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:10.764
It was designed to leave you not
in a real place.

00:07:11.348 --> 00:07:15.560
Carter Burwell:
Gravity isn't quite working
right and you're unsettled by this, as a viewer.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:16.061
(Screaming)

00:07:16.061 --> 00:07:17.562
(Thunderous knocking)

00:07:17.562 --> 00:07:22.442
♪♪

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:27.906
Cydney Cornell:
What we're all doing is
contributing to a framed piece of art that Joel is creating.

00:07:27.906 --> 00:07:31.368
What we're creating with the
costumes were, sort of, chess
pieces.

00:07:31.368 --> 00:07:35.121
All the styles, the costumes and
everything, were just solid
edges.

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:39.084
Mary Zophres:
Shapes, triangular, conical,
cylinder.

00:07:39.084 --> 00:07:42.420
And the way people, sort of,
moved around the set were like
chess characters.

00:07:42.837 --> 00:07:45.965
Mary Zophres:
I based it on things that would
be flattering first

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:48.760
Mary Zophres:
to Frances McDormand and to
Denzel Washington.

00:07:49.094 --> 00:07:54.974
That's one of the reasons why
there's lattice work in the
chest and on the shoulders of the armor.

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:58.895
Mary Zophres:
And there's this tufting
technique that we use on the under-armor.

00:07:58.895 --> 00:08:01.690
Mary Zophres:
And a quilting technique that we
used in the sleeves.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:06.528
Mary Zophres:
And it's all in an effort to
create interest without being distracting in close-ups.

00:08:06.528 --> 00:08:08.238
Bruno Delbonnel:
And then it was about shapes.

00:08:08.238 --> 00:08:11.366
Bruno Delbonnel:
How can I bring some tension to
the image?

00:08:11.366 --> 00:08:13.243
Yes!
(Smashing)

00:08:13.243 --> 00:08:18.331
Yet here's a spot. Out, damned
spot! Out, I say!

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:22.794
But it was, uh, trying to find
balance where they have to work
together

00:08:22.794 --> 00:08:25.672
Bruno Delbonnel:
the lines and the acting and
the light are in sync.

00:08:26.381 --> 00:08:31.469
He, kinda, gives a very clear
idea of what the lighting is
supposed to be on set.

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:35.390
Michael Huber:
And that, kinda, dictates also
what we're gonna be working with later.

00:08:35.682 --> 00:08:38.768
So, whatever we do needs to fit
into his lighting scheme.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.604
Stefan Dechant:
It was also Bruno's idea, which
I thought was fantastic,

00:08:41.604 --> 00:08:43.940
Stefan Dechant:
oh, let's start painting the
shadows into the sets.

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:51.156
All men else I have avoided
thee, but get thee back, my soul
is too much charged with blood of thine already.

00:08:51.156 --> 00:08:52.741
(Thunderous knocking)

00:08:52.741 --> 00:08:57.162
♪♪

00:08:57.162 --> 00:09:00.915
Frances is brilliant,
obviously, and passionate.

00:09:01.458 --> 00:09:03.126
Denzel Washington:
I love her. She's a beast

00:09:03.126 --> 00:09:04.753
and so am I.

00:09:04.753 --> 00:09:08.548
So, it was a great, uh,
we wouldn't even call it a
battle because it was a concert.

00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:10.759
It's just two forces coming
together,

00:09:11.301 --> 00:09:12.385
I was getting ready to say for
good,

00:09:12.844 --> 00:09:14.637
but not for good, for evil.

00:09:14.888 --> 00:09:15.889
But, we did it well.

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:20.310
I don't think it gets better
than working with Bruno as a DP.

00:09:20.769 --> 00:09:25.356
Stefan Dechant:
What Bruno brings to it is just
this incredible cinematic history.

00:09:25.356 --> 00:09:27.984
Stefan Dechant:
I mean, he's a walking
encyclopedia.

00:09:27.984 --> 00:09:30.862
Denzel Washington:
I had a wonderful relationship
with the cinematographer

00:09:31.196 --> 00:09:32.864
Denzel Washington:
because he couldn't beat me to
work.

00:09:32.864 --> 00:09:35.658
If he was there at five, and I'm
like, "Man, I've been here two
hours."

00:09:36.201 --> 00:09:39.204
Denzel Washington:
I would do those speeches and
soliloquies just over and over.

00:09:39.537 --> 00:09:45.418
Our collaboration with Bruno was
far closer this time than it 
was on, on the last movie

00:09:45.418 --> 00:09:49.089
because he was involved a lot
more in the, in the post
process.

00:09:49.089 --> 00:09:50.757
Well, it was fun working with
them

00:09:50.757 --> 00:09:52.550
and they, they were fabulous.

00:09:52.967 --> 00:09:57.222
He would bounce ideas off of us
and ask things that he would
like to try out

00:09:57.222 --> 00:09:58.807
Michael Huber:
and see what stuff looks like.

00:09:59.182 --> 00:10:03.770
So, it was a, kind of, an
ongoing process. I said "Oh, why
don't we do this or that?"

00:10:04.062 --> 00:10:07.524
Alex and Michael, uh, the East
Side Effects guys,

00:10:07.982 --> 00:10:10.652
Lucian Johnston:
they have a collaboration with
Joel and they've been,

00:10:10.652 --> 00:10:13.655
Lucian Johnston:
I think this is like their
third or fourth movie with him.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:18.034
Lucian Johnston:
On this movie, we started
turning stuff over to them almost immediately.

00:10:18.368 --> 00:10:23.748
And that really, really helped
us because there are so many
sequences in the film

00:10:23.748 --> 00:10:27.961
Lucian Johnston:
where it's, kind of, a
conversation with the VFX department.

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:33.466
Lucian Johnston:
We couldn't finish cuts of
scenes until the VFX were, kind of, at a certain place.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.555
Lucian Johnston:
And so the VFX had to, kind of,
inform the edit of certain scenes.

00:10:38.555 --> 00:10:43.268
Lucian Johnston:
So, we had to, kind of, go 
back-and-forth with them a lot, and they really made that easy.

00:10:43.268 --> 00:10:44.727
Ahh! (Grunt)

00:10:44.727 --> 00:10:49.816
You remember in the
apparition room, with the three
witches are talking to him?

00:10:49.816 --> 00:10:54.195
The cauldron is, obviously we
didn't have water on set.

00:10:54.195 --> 00:10:56.739
So the cauldron is totally
visual effect.

00:10:56.739 --> 00:11:01.327
Alex Lemke:
Working together with the DP,
Bruno Delbonnel, was a great experience.

00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:04.622
Alex Lemke:
For me, this time was the first
one where he was actually able 

00:11:04.622 --> 00:11:07.292
Alex Lemke:
to work completely in a sound
stage.

00:11:07.292 --> 00:11:10.003
Alex Lemke:
Which he's known for, because he
likes to control the lighting 

00:11:10.003 --> 00:11:11.629
Alex Lemke:
to the nth degree.

00:11:11.629 --> 00:11:16.134
So, here, he could really shine
and show us what he's capable
of.

00:11:16.134 --> 00:11:18.970
Bruno Delbonnel:
We tried to do as much in camera
as we could.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:23.933
Bruno Delbonnel:
It was not like bringing
multiple cameras and like 3D thing.

00:11:25.393 --> 00:11:29.188
Bruno Delbonnel:
It was just like, no, one camera
then we sort it out 

00:11:29.188 --> 00:11:30.356
later in post.

00:11:30.648 --> 00:11:35.320
And, I think there ended up
being over like 400 VFX shots.

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:38.615
Lucian Johnston:
Which, for a film of this size,
is a lot.

00:11:38.615 --> 00:11:40.450
Some are very complex.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:45.204
The end, the crows and
everything, is all visual
effects.

00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:46.915
They did an amazing job.

00:11:46.915 --> 00:11:49.792
(Cawing)

00:11:49.792 --> 00:11:51.878
♪♪

00:11:51.878 --> 00:11:57.717
The opportunity to work with
Joel and Frances on this
project, in particular,

00:11:57.717 --> 00:11:59.594
Denzel Washington:
was one I couldn't miss.

00:11:59.969 --> 00:12:02.263
To follow Joel and to see how he
works and

00:12:02.263 --> 00:12:05.808
the level of concentration and
preparation was a sight to see.

00:12:05.808 --> 00:12:08.519
So, it was one of the best
experiences I've had.

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:12.482
Stefan Dechant:
As an auteur, Joel surrounded
himself with these other artists

00:12:12.482 --> 00:12:17.862
Stefan Dechant:
that wanted nothing more but to
participate in his creation of this world.

00:12:17.862 --> 00:12:21.824
And I think that as an audience
member, I hope that you see
that.

00:12:22.158 --> 00:12:26.371
I'm really proud of it.
I think it's enormously, uh,
beautiful.

00:12:27.246 --> 00:12:28.581
I feel like I've done my job
well.

00:12:28.581 --> 00:12:29.958
It was very special.

00:12:29.958 --> 00:12:33.002
The time to worry about flying
is when you're on the ground.

00:12:33.002 --> 00:12:34.837
If you don't trust the pilot,
don't go.

00:12:35.171 --> 00:12:39.092
Denzel Washington:
We were flown around by the
most brilliant pilot

00:12:39.592 --> 00:12:41.010
Denzel Washington:
in the business, Joel Coen.

00:12:41.678 --> 00:12:44.472
Denzel Washington:
And a brilliant co-pilot,
